== 1.3 Influences on Health

Health is shaped by interacting factors: inherited characteristics, age, working and living conditions, education, income, safe water, sanitation, housing, social support and access to healthcare. Individual actions matter, but they do not operate independently of these conditions. For example, advice to wash hands is harder to follow consistently when a school lacks water and soap.

A *risk factor* is associated with a greater chance of an outcome; it is not proof that an individual will develop it. A *protective factor* can reduce risk or support wellbeing. Association alone does not prove that one factor caused a particular person's condition. Health promotion can combine accurate information with practical changes such as providing safe facilities and removing barriers to care.

#concept-box("Individual and environment")[
  A class lesson about handwashing builds knowledge. Installing and maintaining a handwashing station supports the opportunity to put that knowledge into practice. Effective programmes often need both.
]

== 1.4 Reliable Information and Seeking Help

Consider the source, date, evidence and purpose of a health claim. Official public-health guidance and appropriately qualified health professionals are generally more reliable than anonymous claims promising a universal cure. A source can still be outdated or unsuitable for a particular individual; check whether advice applies to the person's age, condition and location.

Seek professional advice for persistent or concerning symptoms. Severe symptoms may require urgent help; follow local emergency procedures. Respect privacy and avoid stigmatising people who seek care. This study book is educational and does not replace individual clinical assessment.

== 1.5 Four Worked Examples
#worked-example(1, "Dimensions of health", tier: "FOUNDATION",
  [A learner asks which dimension is most directly concerned with relationships and belonging. \
A. Social wellbeing \
B. Bone length \
C. Pulse counting \
D. Joint classification],
  [Relationships and belonging are aspects of social wellbeing. The answer is *A*.])
#worked-example(2, "A barrier to hygiene", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [A school teaches handwashing but has no soap or water. Which response best addresses both knowledge and access? \
A. Repeat the lesson without addressing facilities \
B. Maintain a safe handwashing station and reinforce the lesson \
C. Assume every pupil can obtain water independently \
D. Stop teaching hygiene],
  [Information alone does not supply materials. Providing and maintaining a station alongside teaching addresses both barriers. The answer is *B*.])
#worked-example(3, "Risk and certainty", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [A factor is associated with increased risk of an illness. Which conclusion is justified? \
A. Every exposed person will develop it \
B. The factor proves the cause of each individual case \
C. The probability may be higher, but the outcome is not certain \
D. Other factors cannot matter],
  [Risk describes probability, not an inevitable result or a complete explanation of an individual case. The answer is *C*.])
#worked-example(4, "Evaluating a claim", tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
  [An undated advertisement claims that one product cures every disease and discourages medical care. The best response is to \
A. accept the claim because it is confident \
B. stop all prescribed treatment immediately \
C. share it without checking \
D. verify evidence and consult an appropriately qualified professional],
  [A universal cure claim and advice to avoid care are warning signs. Check reliable current evidence and seek appropriate advice rather than changing treatment on the basis of an advertisement. The answer is *D*.])

#trap-box("Hygiene is not a guarantee")[
  Good hygiene reduces some risks but cannot prevent every illness. Do not infer that an ill person must have poor hygiene or that an apparently healthy person needs no assessment.
]

